WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it was june 23 2019 and i was visiting  the zetree refugee camp in jordan  and the zitri camp is an interesting  sight to see because across the horizon  there in the middle eastern desert where  ancient civilizations once lived is a  makeshift settlement of row after row  after row of corrugated metal trailer  and this is where the folks in zachary  live  often they have paintings on the side  drawn by children in the camp  and on that day i was visiting with a  colleague of mine who works for save the  children jordan and we got to meet a  friend of mine named esma  we visited esma's trailer and we knocked  on the wooden door and esthema kindly  greeted us as she always does with such  great kindness and we walk in and we sit  on the dark brown carpet on a dark brown  cushion with the blue flowers all around  the trailer  and we sit and we drink tea together shy  in arabic the shy that she had prepared  for us  and as we were drinking our tea esma was  talking to us about her life in the camp  her life back in syria and the great  work she's doing in the camp suddenly  there was a loud noise around us and so  the trailer wall started rumbling it  felt like the ground was shaking  and finally i looked over to my  colleagues as i looked over to esma said  something going on and esma said yes  just wait  and in that moment 10 children burst  through that wooden door we had come  through onto the ground and the carpet  and the brown carpet in front of us  and rolled around and and played with  one another and the the room had become  chaos  and just a few seconds later  they each stand up give esma a big hug  and greet her esma says hello to each of  them by name  a greeting in arabic  she says it to each of them and then  finally they sit down in a semi-circle  around esma and esma sits with them  and she grabs a hold of a children's  book that was there  and suddenly after grabbing her baby boy  muhammad who had been crawling all over  the carpet putting her putting him  firmly in her lap  she begins the story and it's as if like  that a magical ambiance had taken over  the trailer  all the kids are so focused in they're  locked in they're listening to  everything esma has to say as she reads  the pages of the book and the dialogue  she reads with her voice moving up and  down like that of an actress to make  sure the kids are engaged  and this book in particular is one about  a syrian boy who  in his words wants to be a pilot of love  not a pilot of war  so this goes on for some time esmera  reads a page of the book and then uh she  pivots the book so that she can show it  to everybody uh all the kids the  illustrations in the book and the kids  lean in and they're they're eager their  eyes wide to see what was on the page  that esma had just described  and finally after about five ten minutes  of reading  asthma closes the book suddenly  it says el nihaya which means the end in  arabic  the magical ambiance is gone and and the  kids are going back to their raucous  behaviors they're playing with one  another they're hanging out but and they  they head out the door eventually but  not before they give esthema one more  big hug  one more greeting to let them know to  let her know how much they love her  esma is a poet author entrepreneur and  storyteller in the zutri refugee camp in  jordan  but it took a lot for her to get there  esma grew up in the town of  syria  where she  lived her life of relative peace  and stability with her with her family  that ended in 2012 when the syrian war  began and when her house was burned to  the ground along with all of her  belongings including the journals that  she had written her whole life's  memories and stories about  and then she crossed the desert to go to  the zetra camp in jordan and within two  years of her time in the camp  came the darkest moment of her life  which is the miscarriage of her child  due largely to a lack of readily  accessible health care  he was in the middle of this darkness  the darkest moment of her life that espa  found the spark within her  the spark being this children's  storytelling initiative that i described  esma wanted to make sure that not a  single child in the zachary camp not one  child  felt as though they did not have a  future that these children who had dealt  with words like war violence bombings  shellings for too long  and who had grown up knowing very little  other than the zachary camp  would have hope for a future would have  the ability to dream  and would feel confident and and  empowered in who they are and that's why  esma started this storytelling  initiative that now brings dozens and  dozens of children to her trailer each  month each week  esma is one of the most inspiring people  i have ever met in my life  but when we hear the stories of refugees  in the media and from the mouths of many  politicians  we don't hear esma's story  we tend to hear the stories of victims  or of villains two binary stories  either we hear the story of victims  people who  are looked down upon should be looked  down upon  why because they don't have agency  because they're pitiful human beings who  need to be given a handout  or we're told the story of villains  why do we look down upon refugees  because they're criminals they're going  to take our jobs and they're going to  ruin our our country's cultures if  they're welcomed in  either way we're told to look down at  refugees  in reality refugees could not be further  from these tropes that are that i just  described and these refugees are  powerful  creative inspiring people some of the  most resilient  innovative human beings on the planet  refugees contribute economically  the us government found that in the 10  years ending in 2014 the net fiscal  impact of refugees on the american  economy was about 63 billion dollars  that's above and beyond the investment  it takes  the small investment but mighty  investment it takes to bring refugees  into the country  a small investment that would pay  dividends but one that has not done  enough especially as resettlement  centers have been decimated over the  last few years  refugees are some of the most  entrepreneurial people on earth and the  most extreme entrepreneurs they're 1.5  to 2x more entrepreneurial than native  born citizens in the us  and they create jobs millions of jobs  for for people around the world in their  new adopted countries and in camps like  zetri  beyond economics socially refugees  are there for our most difficult times  so when hurricane harvey hit houston it  was the afghan cultural center and many  others of course that was providing aid  to firemen firefighters firemen and  women who were helping ensure that  people were protected and safe and  provided with food and water and what  they needed  in utica when utica new york  when there were factory closures across  the rust belt and when a town that was  once fast growing was dwindling in  population as people abandoned it  it was refugees from 40 countries that  rekindled the spirit there  that repopulated churches and mosques  that brought hope to a city that was  declining rapidly and is now stabilized  and most recently during this pandemic  refugees have provided food for the  elderly changing their small businesses  and and  into opportunities to serve uh folks who  are most disadvantaged during the  pandemic providing food and and  equipment that's necessary  but more importantly than what refugees  contribute is who refugees are  equal human beings with dignity that  deserve to be treated with respect just  like all of us  as one poet says no one chooses refugee  camps  they didn't choose their situations and  it could have happened to any of us  they're equal human beings who deserve  respect independent of their  contributions  and i believe we all deep down know this  we know that refugees and all other  people different than us are equal human  beings who deserve respect  but there's a disconnect  because when polled the majority of  americans have said in the past that  they do not want to welcome  syrian refugees to this country at all  or they only want to welcome syrians  from certain religions and backgrounds  and in some ways this disconnect  actually  is intuitive it's very sad but it's  intuitive because in the absence of  knowing a syrian or knowing a refugee or  knowing someone in any group that is  different than us we have to fill in the  gaps  and where do we fill in the gaps we fill  in the gaps from the cacophony of sounds  and media clips and politicians speeches  and headlines and politicized things  that are all over the media that tell us  the victim or the villain narrative  not the narrative that says  these are beautiful human beings  with with lives of dignity that are  being played out in communities we don't  hear the story of esma we don't hear the  story of utica we don't hear the story  of houston after harvey  my hope is for three things  most urgently my hope is that the world  takes its duty that it committed to  after world war ii and welcomes refugees  into their countries there are 27  million refugees in the world right now  a number that has gone up over the last  week as half a million ukrainians  have huddled together in train stations  to exile out of their country  because of the horrendous violence  that's occurring in ukraine  my hope is that  countries band together and commit to  welcoming refugees because right now we  are failing miserably  only a shocking 0.2 percent of refugees  in the world are resettled because  nations are failing to accommodate and  to welcome them  beyond that my hope is that there are  more stories that allow us to look past  the victim or villain narratives more  authentic human stories that bring the  voices of people like esma into our  lives  my book that's coming out in may 25  million sparks is meant to tell the  untold story of refugee entrepreneurs  including esma and i hope it contributes  to that  but beyond the stories  i have a greater hope  and that hope is that each of us when  we're faced with these stories and  narratives  about who refugees are or who people of  different races or ethnicities or  religions than us are  we stop and we look within  and we remember  that yes everyone is equal to us and  that their differences should be honored  and respected and that everyone deserves  to be treated with dignity just the way  esma treats those wonderful children  in her trailer during her storytelling  initiatives in the camp  and that everyone everyone is a powerful  beautiful creative human being who  deserves to be treated with equal  respect and love  thank you  [Music]

